From: conda-forge hkl-feedstock maintainers
Date: 2026-05-26
Subject: [ccan] Make configurator cross-compile-aware

`hkl/ccan/configurator.c` (vendored from ccan) is a build-time helper
that probes a compiler's feature set (typeof, __attribute__ variants,
HAVE_MMAP, HAVE_UCONTEXT, ...) and emits `hkl/ccan/ccan_config.h` for
the rest of the ccan code to gate on.

For each probe, configurator compiles a small program and (for
EXECUTE/INSIDE_MAIN style probes) RUNS the resulting binary. In a
cross-compilation environment (e.g. conda-forge building osx-64
packages on a linux-64 host) two things go wrong:

1. The `configurator` binary itself is built by automake's
   `noinst_PROGRAMS=configurator` machinery, which uses $(CC) -- the
   target cross-compiler. The resulting Mach-O binary cannot be
   exec'd on the build host:

       ./configurator x86_64-apple-darwin13.4.0-clang > ccan_config.h.tmp
       /bin/sh: line 1: ./configurator: cannot execute binary file:
                                        Exec format error

2. Even after fixing (1), the probes compile target-arch binaries
   that likewise cannot be exec'd on the build host:

       ./configurator: Test for HAVE_ALIGNOF failed with 32256:
       sh: line 1: ./configurator.out: cannot execute binary file:
                                       Exec format error

Fixes:

* Compile `configurator` itself with $(CC_FOR_BUILD), falling back
  to $(CC) when CC_FOR_BUILD is unset (native builds).
* Pass `--configurator-cc=$(CC_FOR_BUILD)` to the running
  configurator so the probe programs are also compiled (and
  therefore runnable) on the build host. ccan's configurator already
  supports this flag.

Caveat for upstream review:
  The probes that depend on the *target* OS (HAVE_UCONTEXT,
  HAVE_MMAP, HAVE_GETPAGESIZE, ...) will now reflect the build-host
  OS, not the target. For Linux->macOS cross-builds these all happen
  to agree (both have ucontext, mmap, getpagesize), so the result
  is correct in practice. For Linux->Windows cross-builds this
  would NOT be correct, and a proper fix there will need pre-canned
  answers via `--var-file=` or a cross-compile launcher. This patch
  is sufficient for macOS and is offered as a stepping stone toward
  full cross-compile support.

Native (non-cross) builds are unaffected: with CC_FOR_BUILD unset,
both the configurator build rule and the --configurator-cc= argument
expand to use $(CC), identical to the prior behavior.

--- a/hkl/ccan/Makefile.am
+++ b/hkl/ccan/Makefile.am
@@ -1,9 +1,19 @@
 AM_CFLAGS=-I$(top_srcdir)
 
-noinst_PROGRAMS=configurator
+# `configurator` is a build-time helper: it runs on the build host and
+# probes the target compiler's features. It must be compiled with the
+# build-host compiler so it can be exec'd on the build host even when
+# cross-compiling. Fall back to $(CC) when CC_FOR_BUILD is unset
+# (native builds), preserving the original behavior in that case.
+CC_FOR_BUILD ?= $(CC)
+configurator: configurator.c
+	$(CC_FOR_BUILD) $(CFLAGS_FOR_BUILD) -I$(top_srcdir) -o $@ $<
 BUILT_SOURCES=ccan_config.h
+# --configurator-cc tells configurator to also compile its probe
+# programs with CC_FOR_BUILD, so probe binaries are runnable on the
+# build host. See recipe/patches/README.md for caveats.
 ccan_config.h: configurator
-	$(builddir)/configurator $(CC) $(CCAN_CFLAGS) > $@.tmp && mv $@.tmp $@
+	$(builddir)/configurator --configurator-cc=$(CC_FOR_BUILD) $(CC) $(CCAN_CFLAGS) > $@.tmp && mv $@.tmp $@
 noinst_LTLIBRARIES=libccan.la
 libccan_la_SOURCES = \
 	ccan_config.h \
